# Dates from engine/notation/chronicle.ts: Uruk at its peak about 3100 BC, perhaps 50,000 people;
# the Great Pyramid about 2560 BC; Mohenjo-daro built about 2500 BC; the Shang about 1600 to 1046 BC.
lesson history-the-first-cities v=1 format=teach grade=3 unit=4 subject=history levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "The first cities"
  goal "Find the first cities on the world map, put them in order, and say what a city needed that a village did not."

  look {
    scene 36x17 {
      worldmap map names=0 blank=-1 oceans=0 places=["Uruk", "Memphis", "Mohenjo-daro", "Anyang"] numbered=0 route=0 at=canvas(1, 0)
    }
    say """
      More than five thousand years ago the first cities grew up beside four great rivers. Uruk,
      between the Tigris and the Euphrates, may have held 50,000 people around 3100 BC. Beside the
      Nile the kings of Egypt ruled from Memphis and built the Great Pyramid. On the Indus stood
      Mohenjo-daro, with its straight streets and drains, and by the Yellow River the Shang kings
      ruled from Anyang. In a city, not everyone farmed.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked history.city-continent k=0
    }
    show history.city-continent k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=4
    show history.cities-order k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.city-jobs k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.city-bc-gap k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.city-continent k=3 easy-k=2 hard-k=5
    show history.cities-order k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show history.city-jobs k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show history.city-bc-gap k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    level hard {
      show history.city-jobs k=3 level=medium
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show history.why-rivers k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show history.villages-in-a-city k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  remember {
    say "The first cities grew beside great rivers, whose farms fed people who did other jobs."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is that cities began in several places at about the same time, each beside a great
    river whose farms could feed thousands of people who did not farm: builders, potters, smiths
    and scribes. The way in finds a city's continent on the map; the core orders the first cities,
    names the new jobs, and counts years BC between them. The wrong idea to watch for is adding
    two BC dates instead of taking one from the other. If the child is stuck on the map, trace the
    river each city stands by. Off the page, find your own town's river, and ask why the town grew
    where it did. England's key stage 2 history asks for "the achievements of the earliest
    civilizations: an overview of where and when the first civilizations appeared", naming Ancient
    Sumer, the Indus Valley, Ancient Egypt and the Shang Dynasty of Ancient China.
    Japan's 社会 takes up history only through Japan's own story, in grade six
    (「我が国の歴史上の主な事象について，人物の働きや代表的な文化遺産を中心に」, the course of study as read in its 2008 edition), so
    this lesson has no line of its own there; its timeline and source skills serve that grade.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a worked example finds Uruk's continent, the cities to order are nearly in order, the jobs are the plainest (potters, bakers), and the years BC are far apart."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the cities to order are fully muddled, the jobs are the new ones (smiths, judges), the years BC are close together, and the three-star villages are smaller."
  }
}
